# Flow References

Flow expressions map data into step inputs and the final return value. A
reference is data, not an Elixir function call. The main reference primitives
are `input`, `context`, `value`, `result`, and `select`.

Run the code cells from top to bottom.

## Install Jido Action

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido_action, "~> 3.0"}])
```

## Define An Echo Action

```elixir
defmodule FlowReferences.Echo do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "flow_references_echo",
    schema: Zoi.map(),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  @impl true
  def run(params, _context), do: {:ok, params}
end
```

## Read Flow Input With `input`

`input(path)` reads the map passed as the Flow input. A path can be an atom,
string, integer, or list of those segments.

```elixir
defmodule FlowReferences.InputExample do
  use Jido.Flow,
    name: "flow_references_input",
    schema: Zoi.map(),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  flow do
    echoed =
      step(FlowReferences.Echo,
        with: %{
          atom_path: input(:account),
          nested_path: input([:account, :id]),
          string_path: input(["account", "id"])
        }
      )

    return(echoed)
  end
end

{:ok, result} =
    Jido.Exec.run(
    FlowReferences.InputExample,
    %{"account" => %{"id" => "acct-1"}},
    %{}
  )

%{atom_path: %{"id" => "acct-1"}, nested_path: "acct-1", string_path: "acct-1"} = result
```

Map lookup accepts atom keys and can also find a string key when the path
segment is an atom. List paths use non-negative integer segments.

## Read Runtime Context With `context`

`context(path)` reads the separate runtime context map. Use it for caller-owned
metadata such as a tenant or trace identifier.

```elixir
defmodule FlowReferences.ContextExample do
  use Jido.Flow,
    name: "flow_references_context",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{value: Zoi.integer()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  flow do
    echoed =
      step(FlowReferences.Echo,
        with: %{
          value: input(:value),
          tenant_id: context([:tenant, :id]),
          trace_id: context(:trace_id)
        }
      )

    return(echoed)
  end
end

{:ok, %{value: 3, tenant_id: "tenant-1", trace_id: "trace-1"}} =
  Jido.Exec.run(
    FlowReferences.ContextExample,
    %{value: 3},
    %{tenant: %{id: "tenant-1"}, trace_id: "trace-1"}
  )
```

Context references do not create graph dependencies. The context exists before
the Flow starts.

## Embed Constants With `value`

`value(term)` stores a literal in the Flow expression. It is useful for fixed
flags, labels, and Action options.

```elixir
defmodule FlowReferences.ValueExample do
  use Jido.Flow,
    name: "flow_references_value",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{value: Zoi.integer()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  flow do
    echoed =
      step(FlowReferences.Echo,
        with: %{
          value: input(:value),
          label: value(:constant),
          options: value(%{mode: :safe})
        }
      )

    return(echoed)
  end
end

{:ok, %{value: 3, label: :constant, options: %{mode: :safe}}} =
  Jido.Exec.run(FlowReferences.ValueExample, %{value: 3}, %{})
```

## Read Prior Results With `result` And Bindings

`result(node, path)` reads a completed node result by its canonical name. In
the module DSL, a step binding is a shorter way to refer to that result.

```elixir
defmodule FlowReferences.ResultExample do
  use Jido.Flow,
    name: "flow_references_result",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{value: Zoi.integer()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  flow do
    source = step(FlowReferences.Echo, with: %{value: input(:value)})

    copied =
      step(FlowReferences.Echo,
        with: %{
          explicit: result(:source, :value),
          binding: select(source, :value)
        }
      )

    return(copied)
  end
end

{:ok, %{explicit: 9, binding: 9}} =
  Jido.Exec.run(FlowReferences.ResultExample, %{value: 9}, %{})
```

Every result reference creates a dependency from the consuming node to the
referenced node. The `copied` step cannot become ready until `source` finishes.

## Project Values With `select`

`select(source, path)` projects a path from an input, context, or result
expression. Use it to pass one nested value instead of a complete result.

```elixir
defmodule FlowReferences.SelectExample do
  use Jido.Flow,
    name: "flow_references_select",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{value: Zoi.integer()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  flow do
    source =
      step(FlowReferences.Echo,
        with: %{
          payload: %{nested: %{value: input(:value)}},
          items: [value(:first), value(:second), input(:value)]
        }
      )

    projected =
      step(FlowReferences.Echo,
        with: %{
          nested_value: select(source, [:payload, :nested, :value]),
          list_item: select(source, [:items, 1])
        }
      )

    return(projected)
  end
end

{:ok, %{nested_value: 6, list_item: :second}} =
  Jido.Exec.run(FlowReferences.SelectExample, %{value: 6}, %{})
```

## Missing Paths Resolve To `nil`

When a map key or list element is missing, path resolution returns `nil`. It
does not raise an exception.

```elixir
defmodule FlowReferences.MissingPath do
  use Jido.Flow,
    name: "flow_references_missing",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{value: Zoi.integer()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  flow do
    source = step(FlowReferences.Echo, with: %{value: input(:value)})

    return(%{
      missing_input: select(input(:missing), :nested),
      missing_result: select(source, [:missing, :nested])
    })
  end
end

{:ok, %{missing_input: nil, missing_result: nil}} =
  Jido.Exec.run(FlowReferences.MissingPath, %{value: 1}, %{})
```

## Shape Maps And Lists

Expressions can be nested in maps and lists. The shape is preserved when the
Flow resolves the expression.

```elixir
defmodule FlowReferences.Shaped do
  use Jido.Flow,
    name: "flow_references_shaped",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{value: Zoi.integer()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  flow do
    source = step(FlowReferences.Echo, with: %{value: input(:value)})

    return(%{
      map: %{value: select(source, :value), fixed: value(:fixed)},
      list: [input(:value), select(source, :value), value(:fixed)]
    })
  end
end

{:ok, %{map: %{value: 2, fixed: :fixed}, list: [2, 2, :fixed]}} =
  Jido.Exec.run(FlowReferences.Shaped, %{value: 2}, %{})
```

## Use Scoped Collection And Loop References

Some references exist only inside one element:

| Reference | Valid scope |
| --- | --- |
| `item(path)` | Map and Reduce `with:` expressions. |
| `item_index()` | Map and Reduce `with:` expressions. |
| `item_id()` | Map and Reduce `with:` expressions. |
| `accumulator(path)` | Reduce `with:` expressions. |
| `state(path)` | Loop `with:`, State `update`, and completion conditions. |
| `iteration_index()` | Loop `with:`, State `update`, and completion conditions. |
| `body_result(path)` | Loop `with:`, State `update`, and completion conditions. |

The authoring surface rejects a scoped reference when its runtime value does
not exist. For example, `item()` is not valid in a Map collection expression,
and `state()` is not valid in Loop initial State.

See [Map and Reduce](flow-collections.livemd) and [Loops and
State](flow-loops-state.livemd) for complete examples.

